maak (1.2.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * maak --dry-run completions no longer empties scripts/maak-completion.bash,
    .zsh and .fish.  A dry run prints the commands and writes nothing, which is
    right, but the task wrote the empty output capture returns in dry-run mode
    over the three files.  An empty capture writes nothing now, so a dry run
    leaves the copies on disk alone, and a capture that fails still stops the
    task before it writes.

