maak (1.2.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * maak.scm no longer imports (maak completion).  This repository's Maak file
    is loaded by whichever maak is installed, so a task in it may only use the
    library that release shipped, and 1.2.0 shipped a file that needed the
    module 1.2.0 introduced: `maak test`, and every other task here, failed with
    "no code for module (maak completion)" under any older maak.  maak
    completions now asks the checkout's ./scripts/maak for the three scripts,
    which is the library beside it.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Mon, 23 Nov 2026 14:30:00 +0100

maak (1.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The shell completions are generated from one description of the interface,
    in (maak completion), and ask maak which tasks the project has instead of
    matching regular expressions against maak.scm.  A task a macro defines, one
    a module the Maak file uses brings in, one a task file in JSON, TOML or YAML
    describes and the ones a -f or -C already on the line names are all offered
    now, and a task that asked to be left out of the listings is left out here
    too.  Every option completes, with its value: a file for -f and -e, a
    directory for -C and -L, a layout for --list=, a shell for --completion.
  * --completion SHELL prints the script for bash, zsh or fish and reads no Maak
    file; --list=completion is the layout a completion menu reads: name, tab,
    first line of the docstring.  maak completions regenerates the three
    checked-in scripts, and the suite fails when a copy and the generator
    disagree.
  * -C no longer prints the directory it entered in front of a listing, a plan
    or an explain, and the launcher no longer lets a CDPATH from the environment
    put a path in front of one, so a --list=json document is the JSON and only
    the JSON.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Mon, 23 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0100

