maak (1.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * --list is a table now: a heading naming the file and how many tasks it
    holds, then one entry a task with its name emphasised, its docstring
    aligned on one column, and what it needs first underneath.  A name wider
    than the column keeps a line of its own, so one long task name no longer
    misaligns the file.
  * --list=STYLE picks another layout: long adds the example a task declares,
    plain keeps the name: documentation blocks the table replaced, names is one
    task name per line, and json is what --list --json prints.
  * --list-width N folds a docstring into N columns; without it a docstring is
    printed as it was written, so a code sample or an Args: table a task laid
    out stays laid out.
  * --list prints the listing and nothing else: no banner, no Loading tasks
    from line, and no environment-file lines between the heading and the first
    task.
  * NO_COLOR, set to a non-empty value, turns ANSI styling off for a whole run
    through a new color? parameter in (maak dsl).
  * tasks-table in (maak maak) renders the aligned listing; list-tasks takes
    #:style, #:width and #:file.
  * maak test runs scripts/maak-test, which is bash and guile and never loads
    this checkout's library before the suite does; it chooses the environment
    (the manifest under Guix, the flake under Nix, or the guile on PATH),
    says which one, and takes MAAK_TEST_ENV=guix|nix|plain.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Sat, 21 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

maak (1.0.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The public API is frozen and documented: docs/stability.org names the
    exported bindings of the installed modules, the command line and its exit
    status, the environment variables, the Maak file's shape and the two
    versioned JSON descriptions that the version number is about, the parts
    left out of that promise, and how a deprecation is announced, kept and
    removed.
  * The homepage is https://github.com/jjba23/maak everywhere: maak --version,
    the help screen, the manual page, the installation page, the README, the
    community page, the Guix and Nix packages, the RPM spec and its OBS
    _service, this package's control and copyright files, the Arch recipe, the
    Homebrew formula and the VS Code extension.  Codeberg archived its copy of
    the repository on 2026-11-08, so it can no longer take a patch or an issue,
    and OBS was still fetching its source from it.
  * maak rpm-build no longer dies on a missing
    packaging/opensuse/maak-rpmlintrc: the file exists, and rpmlint is given it
    with -r instead of being trusted to find it beside the RPM.

